The flag of Tonga consists of a red field with a white canton (upper hoist-side rectangle) containing a centered red Greek cross. The resulting matrix therefore takes only the two scalar values \(a\) (red pixels) and \(b\) (white pixels).  

All rows lying strictly below the canton are identical and equal to the constant vector whose every entry is \(a\). Inside the canton the right-hand portion of every row (the part lying outside the canton) is likewise constantly \(a\). The only variation occurs in the left-hand segment of those rows and is produced by the cross, which creates at most three combinatorially distinct patterns:

- rows intersecting neither arm of the cross (left-hand segment entirely \(b\));
- rows intersecting only the vertical arm (left-hand segment contains a centered block of \(a\) whose width equals the arm thickness);
- rows intersecting the horizontal arm (left-hand segment contains a wider centered block of \(a\)).

Consequently the matrix possesses at most four distinct row vectors. Its rank is therefore at most 4.  

Choosing generic scalars \(a\neq b\) makes these four vectors linearly independent, so the bound 4 is attained.  

Thus the maximal possible rank is exactly 4.

