{I believe the true statements are I, II, and III (choice A). 

Twitching motility assays for P. aeruginosa are classically performed via stab inoculation through a thin agar layer (typically ~1% agar) to allow subsurface twitching at the agar/plastic interface, so I is correct. Standard 10 cm plates are poured with ~25 ml agar medium, so II is correct. Swarming is supported on 0.5% agar plates when glycerol is supplied as the carbon source (common in multiple published protocols), so III is correct.

IV is false because metal chelators (e.g., EDTA, lactoferrin) actually promote rather than inhibit swarming in many conditions by lowering iron. V is false because repeated washing removes secreted pigments (pyocyanin/pyoverdine), so the concentrated cell suspension is not blue-green. Hence only I–III hold.}

